      "rows": [
        {
          "dimension": "Supply Chain",
          "level": "High",
          "note": "Compromising a single npm maintainer account can inject malware into packages with billions of weekly downloads, giving attackers asymmetric leverage across the entire crypto developer ecosystem.",
          "source": "https://checkmarx.com/blog/npm-account-takeover-results-in-crypto-supply-chain-attack/"
        },
        {
          "dimension": "Centralization",
          "level": "High",
          "note": "npm's trust model centralizes enormous power in individual maintainer credentials; there is no multi-sig or threshold requirement before a malicious version reaches production users.",
          "source": "https://www.stepsecurity.io/blog/axios-compromised-on-npm-malicious-versions-drop-remote-access-trojan"
        },
        {
          "dimension": "Smart-contract",
          "level": "Medium",
          "note": "npm attacks do not exploit smart-contract logic directly, but backdoored SDKs and wallet libraries can silently redirect transaction signing to attacker-controlled addresses, producing the same fund-loss outcome.",
          "source": "https://xrpl.org/blog/2025/vulnerabilitydisclosurereport-bug-apr2025"
        },
        {
          "dimension": "Regulatory",
          "level": "Medium",
          "note": "Software supply chain security is under increasing regulatory scrutiny following high-profile incidents, but no crypto-specific npm disclosure or remediation mandate yet exists.",
          "source": "https://www.stepsecurity.io/blog/multiple-redhat-cloud-services-npm-packages-compromised"
        },
        {
          "dimension": "Market",
          "level": "Medium",
          "note": "Discovery of a poisoned SDK in a major protocol typically triggers an immediate advisory to avoid all DeFi dApps, causing short-term liquidity flight and reputational damage even when the attack window is narrow.",
